10 May 26
The "Skip k values depending on sieve_list" section of code needs
improvement
The variables that are used to determine the next value of k are:
sieve_vals, sieve_mod, sieve_to and sieve_cur
sieve_vals is the set of integer values to be sieved
sieve_mod is a dictionary which has lists of the values of k
modulo p such that mul.k-1 will be a chain which will not be of
the desired length
sieve_to is a dictionary for which the sieve_vals set contains
values which satisfy the modulo conditions for a specific prime p

There are a number of deficiencies in the current code.
When the next value of k is ready to be checked, the sieve_to
values are checked and if any sieve_to values are less than k,
then for that value of p, then values which correspond to
sieve_mod values for p are added to sieve_vals.
This is inefficient because all the values of the dictionary are
checked
An alternative is to have sieve_to values have a linked list

11 May 26
Have done some reading about python and names and values and
wondering how linked lists might work for me.
If I have a tuple with (p,t) where the t is the sieve_to value
associated with the prime p, then I want a linked list with this
tuple value and a pointer to the next tuple with the next value
of t that needs to be considered.
My algorithm will then have a linked list of (p,t) tuples which
are ordered by t values, lowest to highest.  Then the
determination of the next k value will involved starting at the
head of the linked list and for the linked list items which have
value of t that is less than k, then determine the sieve_vals
that correspond to the values around k and add them to the
sieve_vals set and determine the value t_new (which is now larger
than k) and construct a new tuple (p,t_new) then traverse the
linked list to place the list item with the (p,t_new) tuple value
in the correct position in the linked list.

A ChatGPT session suggested to me that linked lists in python
aren't good.  If I am wanting a sorted list as above, then use a
python list with the bisect module, and reorder the tuple to be
(t,p) so insort can sort by first element in the tuple.

